# Pav reporting color standard

Use the same meaning for every color across Pav Law dashboards, canvases, and reports:

| Meaning | Cursor theme token |
|---|---|
| Cases / new matters | `category.blue` |
| LSA | `category.cyan` |
| Digital ads / Search | `category.orange` |
| Website | `category.pink` |
| Spend / cost | `category.purple` |
| Cash in / credits / positive status | `category.green` |
| Debits / gaps / errors / negative status | `category.red` |
| Neutral or unavailable | `category.gray` or default text |

## Graph rules

1. The same series keeps the same color in every graph, table accent, and KPI.
2. Reserve red and green for semantic negative/positive states; do not use them as arbitrary categories.
3. Use theme tokens only — no hardcoded hex colors.
4. Never communicate by color alone. Include direct labels or a legend; use bars for volume and lines for spend.
5. Keep related charts on the same background and limit visible series to the minimum needed.
6. Do not invent a new palette for an individual chart. Add a new meaning to this standard first.
7. **Time ordering:** chart axes run oldest → newest, left to right, so the most recent period sits on the right edge. Detail-table month rows run current → oldest. Forecast periods extend to the right of actual periods, separated by a divider and drawn at reduced opacity.

Basis: IBM Carbon and AWS Cloudscape data-visualization guidance — fixed categorical colors, consistent series identity, adequate contrast, and non-color cues for accessibility.
